steps.body:
An array holds a fixed number of values of one type. Write the values in braces to create one:

```java
int[] temps = {18, 21, 19};
```

Indexes start at 0, so `temps[0]` is the first value and `temps[2]` is the third. The same bracket syntax reads and writes:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] temps = {18, 21, 19};
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(first);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(temps));
    }
}
```

```java
int first = temps[0];
temps[1] = 25;   // replace the second value
temps[2] += 3;   // compound assignment works too
```

`temps.length` is the number of slots. It is a field, not a method, so it takes no parentheses (`temps.length()` is a compile error). The last index is always one less than the length, so `temps[temps.length - 1]` is the last element for an array of any size.

Gotcha: an index below 0, or equal to `length` or above, throws an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` and stops the program. `temps[temps.length]` is the classic off-by-one mistake.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {18, 21, 19, 24, 22};

        System.out.println("First day: " + temps[0]);
        System.out.println("Last day: " + temps[temps.length - 1]);
        System.out.println("Days: " + temps.length);

        temps[2] += 3;
        System.out.println("Day 3 is now: " + temps[2]);
    }
}

