steps.body:
An array variable does not hold the values. It holds a reference to an array object stored elsewhere. Assigning one array variable to another copies the reference, so both names share one array:

```java
int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = a;   // b points at the same array
b[0] = 99;     // a[0] is now 99 too
```

That is also why `==` on arrays asks "is this the same array object?" and not "are the contents equal?". Here `b == a` is `true`, but two separate arrays that both hold `{1, 2, 3}` are not `==`.

For an independent copy, use `a.clone()` or `Arrays.copyOf(a, newLength)` (import `java.util.Arrays` for `copyOf`). `copyOf` can change the length too: extra slots get default values and a shorter length cuts off the end.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] a = {99, 2, 3};
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(copy));
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(longer));
    }
}
```

```java
int[] copy = a.clone();              // new array, same values
int[] longer = Arrays.copyOf(a, 5);  // [99, 2, 3, 0, 0]
```

Arrays have a fixed size. To grow one, make a bigger copy and point the variable at it with `a = Arrays.copyOf(a, 5)`. Any alias such as `b` still points at the old, shorter array.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] levels = {1, 2, 3};

        int[] alias = levels;
        alias[0] = 99;

        int[] copy = levels.clone();
        copy[1] = 50;

        System.out.println("levels: " + Arrays.toString(levels));
        System.out.println("alias: " + Arrays.toString(alias));
        System.out.println("copy: " + Arrays.toString(copy));
        System.out.println("alias == levels: " + (alias == levels));
        System.out.println("copy == levels: " + (copy == levels));

        levels = Arrays.copyOf(levels, 5);
        System.out.println("grown: " + Arrays.toString(levels));
        System.out.println("alias length: " + alias.length);
    }
}

